#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}?$\n\n- $\\{ -2, 0, 4 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ 0, 3 \\}$\n\n- $\\{ -3, 1, 3 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S.$ Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\le 2n+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4\\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2\\le n & \\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{ -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{ -2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\{ 0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $\\{ -3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{ a, b, c \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/8323a4292a4f06d66d456bfce1906cbc7e9933a3fac41dd5fb643e13d25f6a6e.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.$ Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{ n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{ n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}?$\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$\n\n- $B\\nsubseteq A$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10,$ that is, $n\\geq 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5,$ that is, $n\\geq 3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A.$\n\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afcd0c4a-83d2-5752-ae51-e865413f5934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r},{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366679} {:answer/id #uuid "cec42cfe-6eae-589b-a99f-3ef70776533b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366680} {:answer/id #uuid "376440eb-7d5f-5251-a663-e5d5a4ce50cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366681} {:answer/id #uuid "94674723-4ab3-52de-85dc-511572ad4dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366682} {:answer/id #uuid "03112bdb-2e89-56ab-beef-902da9572cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94674723-4ab3-52de-85dc-511572ad4dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{r},{t},{r,t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366682} :answer-field/id #uuid "b48d7cc0-deda-54c4-a5a3-ddc79936f9e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17cce24b-1209-590f-8569-68896def4649" :question/math-academy-id "q-104924" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $S={r,t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $S,$ we list out every combination of elements within $S.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/022399e6a7a5e1f79b58e856aa8e9e5e9f3561e8e4bcb5b80a95237d30d06002.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{r},{t},{r,t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2ff121-820b-52c2-b693-d6ed16de88ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{1},{-1,0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300896} {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fea61a-e1a7-5363-bed2-b0a8632c866b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0},{-1},{1},{-1,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300898} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2d551e-b3f4-522e-9001-14569ed6604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300899} {:answer/id #uuid "3bee4e3f-eba6-5c0e-8b3e-45c07d7bbf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{1},{1,2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f5e149-b2c2-5e2d-96a1-353036ed393e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{0},{1},{0,1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dff9a6a-b9cf-56f0-80c2-36ecdda2b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfdf525d-068e-5e8a-a728-e4a1792f0ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104925" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A={0,1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/bb31e2bd632f39706b2785ce6cab764673997fe77b8a9417f7dc213cdff4b7a7.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{0},{1},{0,1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210d5fd3-52a4-5747-be08-1606c454ff88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540} {:answer/id #uuid "78491162-0678-5759-b0c9-bc4216f09025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372541} {:answer/id #uuid "77ace381-105a-5a4c-8283-88d0f8afc465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542} {:answer/id #uuid "d09de186-f91d-5e49-bea5-eb0615dcbbd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372543} {:answer/id #uuid "011f8fa9-da1f-5562-95f3-5fb0340f573f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "011f8fa9-da1f-5562-95f3-5fb0340f573f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372544} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c004aee-b348-5189-ab95-0dc257eda440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ccd9d30-cbcf-53db-8687-8f45cfff87ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-105033" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\n- $Z$\n- ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2e22a02-b744-5948-8be1-8718ed9ade7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366686} {:answer/id #uuid "58576262-6947-542b-ae10-b68d549cff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} {:answer/id #uuid "5f21bb08-5a57-58bd-954b-dd9682883434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366688} {:answer/id #uuid "172622cd-f422-5c25-9ce1-1c3c4507c66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366689} {:answer/id #uuid "36c89114-7b26-5ecd-825e-c7d06dd1d63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58576262-6947-542b-ae10-b68d549cff64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} :answer-field/id #uuid "03816318-2156-51fb-a9d6-f71971bcf414" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d87f299-b49e-50b2-9b8e-197c261ee00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105065" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z∣x\\le 5}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣-7\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $-7\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={-7,-6,-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false.  For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd6c6914-1b53-5c9a-aaa3-cc81b38cc552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372547} {:answer/id #uuid "4a827ea2-e8b1-5186-91d7-2c27c85c4cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,c,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372548} {:answer/id #uuid "836ad776-a89b-5b74-9af1-adfd6b269e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,c,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372549} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe68fbe-52b1-5578-9d17-1d6d3e0ec5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{c,f}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372550} {:answer/id #uuid "29cc7ea2-228f-5665-9b12-4fb701d32d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,c,d,e,f}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe68fbe-52b1-5578-9d17-1d6d3e0ec5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{c,f}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372550} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b0007f-f1ac-5b98-9178-43e61fe70321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79b980a8-b444-5f71-8a39-dcab8f89c437" :question/math-academy-id "q-1188" :question/problem "Which set is a subset of $B={a,b,c,f}?$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $B$ if all its elements are elements of $B.$\n\nOf the given sets, only ${c,f}$ has all of its elements in $B.$\n\nTherefore, ${c,f}$ is a subset of $B.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d44517ee-3d6f-507f-8374-71773c43e79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931} {:answer/id #uuid "196cd7ad-5bf1-5c90-9fea-ae0a5e8795de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300932} {:answer/id #uuid "6805c159-44f8-5f1b-84c6-f9503dadd760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300933} {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} {:answer/id #uuid "0dabb692-b809-5f64-8d4b-add3d249ec5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1,3,5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59339c0a-97aa-5ed7-a9d7-2497c86de036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe02cb1-6b45-55e8-9609-6c2856bed3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d9bbfa7-b5b1-52d4-bc25-0a500880f2e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1189" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={1,2,3,4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nNotice that the empty set ${}$ is a subset of any set.\n\nTherefore, ${}$ is a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9460bfff-6995-5a7c-a039-411642a127cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x},{y},{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372554} {:answer/id #uuid "6144e3b6-0f4b-59f0-bf7c-cdc905872f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{x},{y},{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372555} {:answer/id #uuid "894bff46-3a6e-5abd-a1c9-decc8762692d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z},{x,y,z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372556} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eb1ac5-3412-52ab-b77e-d81ada61488c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{x},{y},{z}{x,y,z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372557} {:answer/id #uuid "f85c7cf7-a584-5c75-a0fe-99051852c189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "894bff46-3a6e-5abd-a1c9-decc8762692d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z},{x,y,z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372556} :answer-field/id #uuid "e88b9c70-cd3c-5d30-bdc1-69db3999a846" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "407d7cc7-5026-5701-afb9-deb48319160f" :question/math-academy-id "q-1191" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A={x,y,z}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/1d7c11586a8fff5f63d46d936fd32c7b5fbfc33a406ddf268bfdd934fe787cdf.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z},{x,y,z}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "254d924b-bee0-53cc-b81e-11f74c39e6f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372561} {:answer/id #uuid "07e403f2-50ae-5018-9532-20b87d3ce2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372562} {:answer/id #uuid "111a0775-0e63-5119-9c08-061621c5a088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{+}⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372563} {:answer/id #uuid "e97d5216-d55b-51c2-bf5a-fbe432e1a433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372564} {:answer/id #uuid "de302136-7644-5210-8f01-fd136225d27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de302136-7644-5210-8f01-fd136225d27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372565} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad001e51-5715-5c03-a6d6-a251356622ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23e8510c-99c3-539c-9c30-63245db2572b" :question/math-academy-id "q-1208" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $C⊆Z$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every complex is an integer number. For example, we have $2\\text{i}\\in C$ but $2\\text{i}\\notin Z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4bea7f3-823b-5a2e-b495-47442814f85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366693} {:answer/id #uuid "d9920768-e99e-5c22-8b0c-0bfd7108317e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{+}⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366694} {:answer/id #uuid "0478eeb5-74b2-5ac3-977d-4d9b667a50dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{-}⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366695} {:answer/id #uuid "1db94549-6799-5eaa-927f-0f288543862b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N⊆Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366696} {:answer/id #uuid "1599f606-1325-5108-8444-b3d33f666c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆{Z}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1599f606-1325-5108-8444-b3d33f666c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z⊆{Z}^{+}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdd904d-b3f1-598e-b1b2-5fd708840195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "274e241e-a0c2-5612-a090-edf98e09aacd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1209" :question/problem "Which statement is **not** true?" :question/worked-solution "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆{Z}^{+}$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a positive integer. For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Z}^{+}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc24b2d-aefe-5550-8320-e73223adc725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051366} {:answer/id #uuid "b21fb707-a876-58e3-8917-4477dbbc31bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051367} {:answer/id #uuid "ece9a89e-b697-565f-9db7-d7d4ea7f957d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051368} {:answer/id #uuid "3d705157-90e1-541c-bdbc-dbf6b491c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051369} {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d45657-6892-5416-b2b9-12620528d63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0906e8f1-346b-507f-9289-d9fb6006311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2346196b-3122-5e48-bad9-803abaca2c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372568} {:answer/id #uuid "db3dca2c-cc00-5fac-9ef0-b9e22ae6f708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372569} {:answer/id #uuid "adfccc75-c1ca-5044-9513-ea6e9a4769eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372570} {:answer/id #uuid "b5db533c-a1e2-51fa-9364-58492fcba2d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372571} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd6fd09-b6ed-59ce-a4b8-ffcb5d35b050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecd6fd09-b6ed-59ce-a4b8-ffcb5d35b050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372572} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5cd6c9e-f9af-52c6-b07a-250d38363d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4922535-c18b-5ded-bfaa-a163a44445c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-1212" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90a1cee1-a065-5c9c-9bac-c5306f1287eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,2,3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372575} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0e4130-3d14-5139-b27d-406e8b1e1c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,2,9,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372576} {:answer/id #uuid "87684fc7-b279-5b9a-be80-4d81fe052fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,5,13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372577} {:answer/id #uuid "84851410-2993-5377-9236-081977f3a3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,5,9,13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372578} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb89e48-f667-564c-b3d3-2aeab4ea2dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,5,9,13,17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87684fc7-b279-5b9a-be80-4d81fe052fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,5,13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372577} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0683d8a-6088-5187-ace9-67a4b3c3cfd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e7e42d7-6551-5aff-864e-31e3a04edb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-37264" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={4n+1∣n\\in {0,1,2,3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {0,1,2,3}$ into the formula $4n+1,$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=0 & \\,⇒\\,4(0)+1=1 \\\\ n=1 & \\,⇒\\,4(1)+1=5 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,4(2)+1=9 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,4(3)+1=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,5,9,13}.\n$$\n\nNow, a subset of $A$ is a set whose elements are also elements of the set $A.$\n\nIn our case, $B={1,5,13}$ is a subset of $A.$\n\nIn the rest of the options, the set $B$ has elements that are not in $A,$ meaning that it is not a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5892d4db-ee17-5a9b-bb7d-4dabe3624e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={0,1,2,4,6,8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} {:answer/id #uuid "87bb18dc-9899-5965-962b-0ce6916f989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={-1,0,1,2,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300946} {:answer/id #uuid "38af7345-898b-5fad-87ca-5b2b33366056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={1,3,5,7,9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300947} {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} {:answer/id #uuid "17e08c17-c0a4-5c01-92e5-c8cea1cf8431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,4,6,7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a1bd9e-7f78-57c3-b75d-8ce5a365964f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={2,6,12,14,32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300948} :answer-field/id #uuid "1864bcf5-a777-5135-8aef-787b82f38f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2265169c-0d8b-562a-b72b-43f80dabce9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37265" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={2n∣n\\in N}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ The elements of the set \n$A$ are all the natural even numbers.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6,8,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, a subset of $A$ is a set whose elements are also elements of the set $A.$\n\nIn our case, $B={2,6,12,14,32}$ is a subset of $A.$\n\nIn the rest of the options, the set $B$ has elements that are not in $A,$ meaning that it is not a subset of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85632a57-e55d-5287-bf45-c249e78e95fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372582} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3a8c3a-e696-5f0c-b5e6-9b1175806bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372583} {:answer/id #uuid "26aac073-2784-58ce-b700-7e1c19837fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372584} {:answer/id #uuid "ef591ac2-93a9-56e6-b3fd-00d41596e791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372585} {:answer/id #uuid "befdbb84-b438-57ae-a9e0-f82275e43739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef591ac2-93a9-56e6-b3fd-00d41596e791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372585} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88c1383-2eeb-507a-a204-683c877bbf7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb98e8f1-5ed6-502a-b031-26657626ffd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37266" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,…}?$\n\n- $B={3n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$\n- $C={6n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3}$\n- $D={2n:n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 4}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $B,C$ and $D.$\n\n- We find the elements of the set $B$ substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $3n,$ and we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,3(1)=3 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,3(2)=6 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,3(3)=9 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,3(4)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $B={3,6,9,12}.$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $C$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3}$ into the formula $6n,$ and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,6(1)=6 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,6(2)=12 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,6(3)=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C={6,12,18}.$\n\n- Finally, the elements of the set $D$ can be found substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3,4}$ into the formula $2n,$ and we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2(1)=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2(2)=4 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2(3)=6 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,2(4)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have, $D={2,4,6,8}.$\n\nNow, a set is a subset of $A$ if all of its elements are also elements of $A.$ With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $B={3,6,9,12}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $C={6,12,18}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- $D={2,4,6,8}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} {:answer/id #uuid "e6865ba6-79cb-5696-ab09-d0be9337b7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300960} {:answer/id #uuid "639e0609-5927-50e2-922f-0b5aa58cc005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300961} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6d2c-da5e-5d94-b324-cd6d72385bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2d22b-0b13-5678-876c-b0cb42f1ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6c89f0-2a1a-5531-b5d8-831e882897bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300959} :answer-field/id #uuid "2783eb9e-a3b8-546f-9d63-ec7ede85ec61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c9ea526-3175-579e-bce7-db2dce376c08" :question/math-academy-id "q-506" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $R?$\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ (the set of positive rationals)\n- ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n- $N$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}^{+}$ is  a subset of $R.$ Every positive rational number is a real number.\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is a subset of $R.$ Every negative integer number is a real number.\n\n- $N$ a subset of $R.$ Every natural number is a real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1654b3-07f9-5637-97a4-44aea15f402a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372589} {:answer/id #uuid "afb75e09-e921-54a9-af65-9ef5cd5a4ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta },{\\alpha ,\\gamma },{\\beta ,\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372590} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9e6ac1-fb02-5635-b9e3-f830328c8485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372591} {:answer/id #uuid "c816e385-3d78-5b83-8df0-203bc1d1183e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\alpha ,\\beta },{\\alpha ,\\gamma },{\\beta ,\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372592} {:answer/id #uuid "99d56e5a-7042-507f-9e1d-13e8d0ad232a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb75e09-e921-54a9-af65-9ef5cd5a4ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta },{\\alpha ,\\gamma },{\\beta ,\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372590} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e24251-c9ec-5552-9365-a835e99ece30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8eba642-fd29-5901-be89-356754671aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80808" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $Q={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $Q,$ we list out every combination of elements within $Q.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/56d0ff0c0e539286b6c15496c7ad295008779f07f4e9132280c787b6303902c0.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta },{\\alpha ,\\gamma },{\\beta ,\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd47303-153d-53c9-8585-16b4eedb092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300980} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceeaee3-0de2-58b2-a573-99d13b8055a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300981} {:answer/id #uuid "15cf81d1-eb58-5d38-b739-c57b9a661a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300982} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} {:answer/id #uuid "884a03de-7ffd-5a19-b4db-73c4a19e7aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d3047d4-2fac-561d-98c5-8e63d74fe94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300983} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ba15e2-1571-5139-a1fb-3c2b08335857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00bdd032-b9a1-5a22-aa27-2be14a896901" :question/math-academy-id "q-80809" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $U={-\\pi ,\\pi }.$" :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $U,$ we list out every combination of elements within $U.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071627/assets/b0e483a2d10cba17fb2bd3bf0e78d7b4d2fc07b22dbb5c492564b998bbfce6fc.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0129e1d7-6042-563d-a3fe-1ac8f1ca778a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372596} {:answer/id #uuid "35d86247-b147-56c7-88a6-73eae54f88f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372597} {:answer/id #uuid "d83ab36c-4ac8-5999-82d9-6f42dc4d4f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372598} {:answer/id #uuid "81ba28d7-1057-5906-9cb0-c9d4ef00c937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372599} {:answer/id #uuid "aa922e21-02ec-586c-8ce4-10c134697700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83ab36c-4ac8-5999-82d9-6f42dc4d4f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372598} :answer-field/id #uuid "081c59a9-4b25-53ae-acfc-c5296c794486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57a9a2fe-7d3b-5760-86ec-db1c77fcf54b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80829" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${R}^{-}$ (the set of negative real numbers)?\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive integers)\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$  (the set of negative rational numbers)" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is *not* a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $0\\in {Z}_{0}^{-}$ but $0\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- $N$ is not  a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ Indeed, every negative rational number is also a negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a9ab72-020d-5396-8333-01a81f55d78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372603} {:answer/id #uuid "48e4bf35-5c06-5b1c-8696-11f535bda248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372604} {:answer/id #uuid "fc09e4cc-7d0a-5fed-8738-20dec76df9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372605} {:answer/id #uuid "123f649c-dde0-556d-91ce-d94189a1a884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372606} {:answer/id #uuid "3e007842-49ad-5fca-b751-2ec8be7d84ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a9ab72-020d-5396-8333-01a81f55d78f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372603} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d8700e1-65a3-5893-ba8a-9a9dea1279f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27ec29c7-bd41-57b6-9534-8283a5c47ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80832" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:1\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<9}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $1$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<9.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943eb759-043f-5bca-9812-c21371e4247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372610} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a2ec3b-6299-5b77-ab29-091cdaa78c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372611} {:answer/id #uuid "44daf9f8-f887-560e-8dc2-1701ac875786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372612} {:answer/id #uuid "32dcc32d-0549-5946-a845-8e14749b97b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372613} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e4f21f-dbaf-5383-94a3-ce1f9a91f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e4f21f-dbaf-5383-94a3-ce1f9a91f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372614} :answer-field/id #uuid "e18bdbef-2c6f-5188-813a-2fbd55072813" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0090583c-ee77-57f6-b6b6-20b90fcbdcc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80837" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in {Z}^{+}∣x\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣0\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $∅⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all positive integers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 3.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $0\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $3\\in A$ but $3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊈B$ is false. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
